WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.061
Picture the office of the company behind a coffee-shop payments app.

2
00:00:07.061 --> 00:00:09.497
It has one small meeting room, room 4.

3
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:12.753
At 11:58, two teams open the booking app.

4
00:00:12.753 --> 00:00:15.593
One books the room from 12:00 to 13:00.

5
00:00:15.593 --> 00:00:17.851
The other, from 12:30 to 13:30.

6
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.633
Both see a free room.

7
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:20.801
Both get Booked.

8
00:00:20.801 --> 00:00:23.795
And at 12:30, both teams are at the door.

9
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:26.013
The app checked before it booked.

10
00:00:26.013 --> 00:00:30.032
It even locked what it found, the fix that stopped the lost update.

11
00:00:30.032 --> 00:00:31.712
So how did this get through?

12
00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:35.143
Let's look at the room the way the database does.

13
00:00:35.143 --> 00:00:39.357
Each booking is a row in a table, with the room and a time range.

14
00:00:39.357 --> 00:00:43.507
Room 4 has one booking this morning: the risk team, nine to ten.

15
00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:49.050
Before it books, the app asks: is there any booking of room 4 that overlaps my slot?

16
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:53.956
And it adds FOR UPDATE, which puts a row lock on every row the question finds.

17
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:57.301
A row lock makes any other writer of that row wait.

18
00:00:57.301 --> 00:00:59.513
That's what stopped the lost update.

19
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.213
So the settlement review team checks 12:00 to 13:00.

20
00:01:03.213 --> 00:01:06.298
A moment later, card ops checks 12:30 to 13:30.

21
00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:09.251
Does card ops' check wait for the first team?

22
00:01:09.251 --> 00:01:10.301
Pause and guess.

23
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:13.692
No.

24
00:01:13.692 --> 00:01:15.720
Both checks find zero rows.

25
00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.527
And a lock goes on the rows a check found, so both lock nothing.

26
00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:24.271
Neither can see the other's booking until it commits.

27
00:01:24.271 --> 00:01:28.503
So both insert, both commit, and 12:30 to 13:00 is booked twice.

28
00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:29.074
Well.

29
00:01:29.074 --> 00:01:31.595
The lock did everything it was asked.

30
00:01:31.595 --> 00:01:33.639
It was asked to guard nothing.

31
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:36.954
Think of the row lock as a very literal guard.

32
00:01:36.954 --> 00:01:40.632
You say: stand next to every booking between twelve and one.

33
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.664
He looks at the sheet, finds none, and stands next to...

34
00:01:44.664 --> 00:01:45.201
nothing.

35
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.198
The other team hires the same guard, with the same orders.

36
00:01:49.198 --> 00:01:53.087
Two guards, both guarding empty space, and two teams walk in.

37
00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:54.239
Map it back.

38
00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:55.984
The guard is the row lock.

39
00:01:55.984 --> 00:01:59.610
The bookings he can stand next to are rows that exist.

40
00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.698
The empty slot is a search that found nothing.

41
00:02:02.698 --> 00:02:05.115
A guard needs something to stand by.

42
00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:07.853
The second booking has a name: a phantom.

43
00:02:07.853 --> 00:02:13.261
It's a row that matches a search, but another transaction wrote it after the search ran.

44
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:20.269
Both the check and the write are about a row that doesn't exist yet, so the clash has nothing to hold on to.

45
00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:23.848
And the stricter setting, Repeatable Read, doesn't save us.

46
00:02:23.848 --> 00:02:26.825
Its snapshot hides the other booking from your reads.

47
00:02:26.825 --> 00:02:29.577
It doesn't stop the other team from inserting it.

48
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:40.178
The same shape turns up wherever a rule says no row like this may exist yet: a username that must be unique, one payout per shop per day, a gift card spent twice.

49
00:02:40.178 --> 00:02:43.701
Each checks that something is missing, then inserts it.

50
00:02:43.933 --> 00:02:46.134
So give the guard something real.

51
00:02:46.134 --> 00:02:50.470
Room 4 has its own row, in the rooms table, and it always exists.

52
00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.337
Lock that first, then check.

53
00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:54.833
Now card ops waits at the room's row.

54
00:02:54.833 --> 00:03:00.345
When the first team commits, card ops checks again, sees 12:00 to 13:00, and doesn't book.

55
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.822
Better still, write the rule into the table: no two bookings of one room may overlap.

56
00:03:05.822 --> 00:03:11.536
PostgreSQL calls this an exclusion constraint, and it checks every booking, committed or not.

57
00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:16.618
The second insert waits for the first, then fails with error 23P01.

58
00:03:16.618 --> 00:03:19.225
There's no check query to get wrong.

59
00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:23.080
Rule of thumb: if the rule fits a constraint, make it one.

60
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:29.123
A lock on a parent row is next best, as long as every path through the code remembers to take it.

61
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.465
So how did two teams book one room?

62
00:03:31.465 --> 00:03:35.124
Each locked what its check found, and its check found nothing.

63
00:03:35.124 --> 00:03:37.484
A lock can only hold a row that's there.

64
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.153
One quick check.

65
00:03:39.153 --> 00:03:45.421
A sign-up page checks that no user named ivy exists, with FOR UPDATE, then inserts her.

66
00:03:45.421 --> 00:03:48.663
Two people sign up as ivy in the same second.

67
00:03:48.663 --> 00:03:50.321
What stops a duplicate?

68
00:03:50.321 --> 00:03:51.473
Pause and think.

69
00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:55.620
Nothing, yet.

70
00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.662
The check finds no row, so it locks no row.

71
00:03:58.662 --> 00:04:01.845
A unique constraint on the username stops it.
